"Driving sales productivity and customer success at OpenAI | OpenAI (https://openai.com/index/openai-gtm-assistant/)\nciteturn3426view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://openai.com/index/openai-gtm-assistant/\",\"lineno\":30}); Total lines: 182\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:   * [Button: Research]\nL3:   * [Button: Products]\nL4:   * [Button: Business]\nL5:   * [Button: Developers]\nL6:   * [Button: Company]\nL7:   * cite1†Foundation(opens in a new window)†openaifoundation.org L8: \nL9: cite2†Try ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com [Button: Login]\nL10: \nL11: OpenAI\nL12: \nL13: September 29, 2025\nL14: \nL15: cite3†API OpenAI on OpenAI\nL16: # Driving sales productivity and customer success at OpenAI\nL17: \nL18: Loading…\nL19: \nL20: Share\nL21: \nL22: Managing growth\nL23: \nL24:   * cite4†Managing growth L25:   * cite5†A teammate, not another tool L26:   * cite6†Defining what “great” looks like L27:   * cite7†Impact you can feel L28:   * cite8†From answers to actions L29:   * cite9†Scaling excellence L30: \nL31:   * cite4†Managing growth L32:   * cite5†A teammate, not another tool L33:   * cite6†Defining what “great” looks like L34:   * cite7†Impact you can feel L35:   * cite8†From answers to actions L36:   * cite9†Scaling excellence L37: This is part of our series sharing internal examples of how OpenAI is using its own technology and APIs. These tools are being used internally, only at OpenAI, and are shared here as illustrative examples of how frontier AI is supporting use cases across our teams. We’re also sharing the internal tool names for a clearer look at how frontier AI helps our teams get work done.\nL38: ## Managing growth\nL39: \nL40: Growth is a good challenge to have, but it comes with pressure. In less than a year, our go-to-market team tripled in size while launching new products almost every week. This pace created a structural challenge: customer demand was increasing, but systems and processes could not keep up.\nL41: Reps often spent an hour preparing for a thirty-minute call, bouncing across dozens of different systems to piece together context. Meanwhile, customers were sending our teams hundreds of product questions weekly, bogging down subject matter experts and slowing deals.\nL42: \nL43: The result: even the most seasoned sellers spent too much time chasing context and answers rather than deepening relationships with customers.\nL44: ## A teammate, not another tool\nL45: \nL46: The team didn’t need another dashboard. They needed a way to reduce preparation overhead and centralize product knowledge.\nL47: \nL48: That idea became GTM Assistant, built on OpenAI’s automation platform and delivered in Slack. It focuses on two friction points:\nL49: \nL50:   * Customer research and preparation: Daily meeting briefs and recaps including account history, call notes, Salesforce activity, and release updates.\nL51:   * Product Q&A: Instant answers sourced from a curated knowledge base, with traceable links back to primary documents.\nL52: \nL53: Rather than a new system to manage, GTM Assistant embedded directly into the reps daily workflows.\nL54: ## Defining what “great” looks like\nL55: \nL56: The breakthrough wasn’t just saving time. It was capturing what the very best salespeople do and sharing it with everyone else.\nL57: Top reps worked directly with GTM Assistant, shaping what “great” looks like in meeting briefs and product responses. Their expertise trained the system. Every eval, correction, and improvement didn’t just sharpen the Assistant, it scaled the habits of the best sellers across the org. Trust is the number one success criteria, which comes from building these solutions hand in hand with the best end users.\nL58: > “We were constantly going back and forth on what does good look like here, really inspecting responses and operationalizing “good” inside of our system.”\nL59: \nL60: Scotty Huhn, GTM Innovation\nL61: \nL62: That hands-on approach became the defining feature of the project. Instead of reps being passive users of a tool, they became designers of a system. Instead of subject matter experts simply answering questions, they became builders of knowledge loops.\nL63: ## Impact you can feel\nL64: \nL65: The results showed up quickly. Today, the average sales rep:\nL66: \nL67:   * Exchanges 22 messages a week with GTM Assistant across daily briefs, recaps, and Q&A\nL68: \nL69:   * Sees a 20% lift in productivity—about one extra day each week to spend with customers and manage a larger book\nL70: \nL71: GTM Assistant also keeps improving. Each week product experts review samples, patch gaps, and push updates back into the system.\nL72: \nL73: “It’s like having a virtual coworker that we re-skill every single week,” says Huhn.\nL74: Customers feel the difference. Questions that once lingered in Slack are answered in minutes. Meetings start sharper, grounded in the latest data and product updates. Deals move faster.\nL75: ## From answers to actions\nL76: \nL77: With trust established, the Assistant is already piloting the next step: logging CRM updates after calls, proactively spotting noteworthy usage patterns, and drafting customer follow-ups to send automatically. Work that used to take hours is starting to happen in the background.\nL78: \nL79: > “The breakthrough was training it on what a successful meeting looks like. Now it acts as an always-on teammate, driving better customer outcomes and a smoother day-to-day for our teams.”\nL80: Scotty Huhn, GTM Innovation\nL81: ## Scaling excellence\nL82: \nL83: The GTM Assistant isn’t about replacing salespeople. It’s about multiplying their impact and driving customer success. By embedding low touch evals into daily workflows, the team built a system where knowledge flows freely, workflows adapt at the speed of deals, and sellers co-own the technology they use.\nL84: \nL85: It shows what’s possible when great salespeople are supported by AI Assistants that turn their excellence into a system that elevates the whole organization.\nL86: ## Ready to put ChatGPT to work in your business?\nL87: \nL88: cite10†Talk with our team L89: ## Keep reading\nL90: \nL91: cite11†View all L92: \nL93: cite12†Turning contracts into searchable data at OpenAI APISep 29, 2025 L94: \nL95: cite13†Converting inbound leads into customers at OpenAI APISep 29, 2025 L96: \nL97: cite14†Empowering teams to unlock insights faster at OpenAI APISep 29, 2025 L98: \nL99: Research\nL100: \nL101:   * cite15†Research Index L102:   * cite16†Research Overview L103:   * cite17†Economic Research L104: \nL105: Latest Advancements\nL106: \nL107:   * cite18†GPT-6.1 Sol L108:   * cite19†GPT-6 Astra L109:   * cite20†GPT-5.6 L110:   * cite21†GPT-5.5 L111: \nL112: Safety\nL113:   * cite22†Safety Approach L114:   * cite23†Deployment Safety(opens in a new window)†deploymentsafety.openai.com L115:   * cite24†Security & Privacy L116:   * cite25†Trust & Transparency L117: \nL118: Products\nL119:   * cite2†ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L120:   * cite26†ChatGPT Business(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L121:   * cite27†ChatGPT Enterprise(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L122:   * cite28†ChatGPT for Education(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L123:   * cite29†Codex†chatgpt.com L124:   * cite30†Dots(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L125:   * cite31†Release Notes L126: \nL127: API Platform\nL128: \nL129:   * cite32†Overview L130:   * cite33†API Log In(opens in a new window)†platform.openai.com L131:   * cite34†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L132: \nL133: Business\nL134:   * cite35†Overview L135:   * cite36†Solutions L136:   * cite37†Resources L137:   * cite38†Plugins L138:   * cite39†Customer Stories L139:   * cite40†Partner Network L140:   * cite10†Contact Sales L141: \nL142: Developers\nL143: \nL144:   * cite41†Apps SDK(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L145:   * cite42†Open Models L146:   * cite43†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L147:   * cite44†Resources(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L148:   * cite45†Developer Forum(opens in a new window)†community.openai.com L149: \nL150: Company\nL151:   * cite46†About Us L152:   * cite47†Our Charter L153:   * cite48†Careers L154:   * cite11†News L155: \nL156: Support\nL157: \nL158:   * cite49†Help Center(opens in a new window)†help.openai.com L159:   * cite50†OpenAI Status(opens in a new window)†status.openai.com L160: \nL161: More\nL162: \nL163:   * cite51†Stories L164:   * cite52†Academy L165:   * cite53†Supply Co. L166:   * cite54†Livestreams L167:   * cite55†Podcast L168:   * cite56†RSS L169: \nL170: Terms & Policies\nL171: \nL172:   * cite57†Terms of Use L173:   * cite58†Privacy Policy L174:   * cite59†Other Policies L175: cite60†(opens in a new window)†x.com cite61†(opens in a new window)†www.youtube.com cite62†(opens in a new window)†www.linkedin.com cite63†(opens in a new window)†github.com cite64†(opens in a new window)†www.instagram.com cite65†(opens in a new window)†www.tiktok.com cite66†(opens in a new window)†discord.gg L176: \nL177: OpenAI © 2015–2026 Your privacy choices\nL178: \nL179: English United States\nL180: \nL181: [Input: Search]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEmpowering teams to unlock insights faster at OpenAI | OpenAI (https://openai.com/index/openai-research-assistant/)\nciteturn3426view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://openai.com/index/openai-research-assistant/\",\"lineno\":30}); Total lines: 185\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:   * [Button: Research]\nL3:   * [Button: Products]\nL4:   * [Button: Business]\nL5:   * [Button: Developers]\nL6:   * [Button: Company]\nL7:   * cite1†Foundation(opens in a new window)†openaifoundation.org L8: \nL9: cite2†Try ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com [Button: Login]\nL10: \nL11: OpenAI\nL12: \nL13: September 29, 2025\nL14: \nL15: cite3†API cite4†ChatGPT OpenAI on OpenAI\nL16: # Empowering teams to unlock insights faster at OpenAI\nL17: \nL18: Loading…\nL19: \nL20: Share\nL21: \nL22: Buried in the noise\nL23: \nL24:   * cite5†Buried in the noise L25:   * cite6†A new way to ask  L26:   * cite7†Making it reliable L27:   * cite8†From tickets to turning points L28:   * cite9†Compounding curiosity L29:   * cite10†The operating model ahead L30: \nL31:   * cite5†Buried in the noise L32:   * cite6†A new way to ask  L33:   * cite7†Making it reliable L34:   * cite8†From tickets to turning points L35:   * cite9†Compounding curiosity L36:   * cite10†The operating model ahead L37: This is part of our series sharing internal examples of how OpenAI is using its own technology and APIs. These tools are being used internally, only at OpenAI, and are shared here as illustrative examples of how frontier AI is supporting use cases across our teams. We’re also sharing the internal tool names for a clearer look at how frontier AI helps our teams get work done.\nL38: ## Buried in the noise\nL39: \nL40: Millions of support tickets come in every year. Each one carries something valuable: a frustration, an idea, a request.\nL41: \nL42: But until recently, those signals were difficult to understand. Dashboards hinted at trends but missed the why. Deep dives took weeks of work from a data scientist. A product leader might want to know how a new feature landed with a unique audience type. But answering required a data scientist to conduct a detailed analysis.\nL43: \nL44: Curiosity was getting rationed.\nL45: “The process required deep technical expertise, and it was cutting off our curiosity,” says Molly Jackman, Head of Business Data.\nL46: ## A new way to ask\nL47: \nL48: We built a research assistant to unlock curiosity that scales. It combines two modes of exploration: dashboards for patterns and a conversational interface for digging deeper. You can start with a chart of trending issues, then ask follow-up questions in plain language.\nL49: We built it by blending what already worked. On one side, classifiers and charts that structured millions of tickets into product areas and themes. On the other, GPT‑5, which could summarize raw tickets and generate flexible reports in plain language. The combination gave us both speed and depth that was simple enough for anyone to use.\nL50: \nL51: “What are healthcare customers saying about new integrations?”\nL52: \nL53: “What’s driving support tickets this quarter?”\nL54: “What major features are hitting the mark?”\nL55: In minutes, the system returns a report sizing the problem, showing prevalence, and highlighting friction points. Leaders no longer have to borrow bandwidth or view static dashboards. Anyone can follow their own questions wherever they lead. For product teams, that means faster iteration on real feedback—knowing what’s working, what isn’t, and drawing clear insights to guide both product launches and long-term roadmaps.\nL56: > “The magic is that you don’t have to predefine your questions, you can just follow your curiosity.”\nL57: \nL58: Molly Jackman, Head of Business Data\nL59: ## Making it reliable\nL60: \nL61: Speed is meaningless without accuracy.\nL62: \nL63: In the early days, ops teams ran manual classifications and data scientists wrote custom models to compare against the assistant. The results lined up.\nL64: \nL65: Over time, confidence grew. Leaders began to cross-check findings against what they were already hearing in the field, and when it matched, they leaned in.\nL66: That cycle—ask, check, trust—turned the assistant into a daily habit for teams. What once took a week of SQL queries and classifiers now happens in a few clicks.\nL67: ## From tickets to turning points\nL68: \nL69: The payoff shows up everywhere.\nL70: \nL71:   * After GPT‑5 launched, product teams had feedback themes in days, not weeks.\nL72: \nL73:   * When the enterprise adoption of connectors slowed, the assistant quickly surfaced the root cause: a buggy onboarding flow. Engineers could then prioritize fixes.\nL74: \nL75:   * In Image generation, it highlighted both the creativity of marketing teams using it for mockups and the friction of rendering delays; two truths that directly shaped the roadmap.\nL76: When the cost of asking a question drops to minutes, more questions get asked. More issues surface. Teams move faster.\nL77: ## Compounding curiosity\nL78: \nL79: The tool doesn’t replace data scientists. It frees them to do different work. Instead of one off analyses, they have more time to  build new classifiers and invest in automation and tooling. Ops teams now generate launch reports in minutes instead of days, freeing capacity to spend more time with customers. Product teams can learn in real time from customers, informing their roadmaps with faster feedback loops.\nL80: ## The operating model ahead\nL81: \nL82: This transformation has shifted how we can listen. Instead of rationing scarce analytical cycles, every team can now pursue their questions freely. Curiosity compounds. A product lead spots a friction point, a sales lead sees the same theme in enterprise tickets, and together they create a faster path to action.\nL83: The hope is that customers feel it most. Issues will get resolved sooner. Features can evolve closer to their needs. Feedback that once sat buried in the backlog is now central to how we build.\nL84: \nL85: > “I think about it as customer UX research at scale. If we’re surfacing the voice of the customer in a way that proactively changes our products, policies, and practices—that’s success.”\nL86: \nL87: Molly Jackman, Head of Business Data\nL88: What began as a tool for parsing millions of tickets is becoming part of the operating system for how we listen. And listening well is how we build well.\nL89: ## Ready to put ChatGPT to work in your business?\nL90: \nL91: cite11†Talk with our team L92: ## Keep reading\nL93: \nL94: cite12†View all L95: \nL96: cite13†Improving support with every interaction at OpenAI APISep 29, 2025 L97: \nL98: cite14†Driving sales productivity and customer success at OpenAI APISep 29, 2025 L99: \nL100: cite15†Converting inbound leads into customers at OpenAI APISep 29, 2025 L101: \nL102: Research\nL103: \nL104:   * cite16†Research Index L105:   * cite17†Research Overview L106:   * cite18†Economic Research L107: \nL108: Latest Advancements\nL109: \nL110:   * cite19†GPT-6.1 Sol L111:   * cite20†GPT-6 Astra L112:   * cite21†GPT-5.6 L113:   * cite22†GPT-5.5 L114: \nL115: Safety\nL116:   * cite23†Safety Approach L117:   * cite24†Deployment Safety(opens in a new window)†deploymentsafety.openai.com L118:   * cite25†Security & Privacy L119:   * cite26†Trust & Transparency L120: \nL121: Products\nL122:   * cite2†ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L123:   * cite27†ChatGPT Business(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L124:   * cite28†ChatGPT Enterprise(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L125:   * cite29†ChatGPT for Education(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L126:   * cite30†Codex†chatgpt.com L127:   * cite31†Dots(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L128:   * cite32†Release Notes L129: \nL130: API Platform\nL131: \nL132:   * cite33†Overview L133:   * cite34†API Log In(opens in a new window)†platform.openai.com L134:   * cite35†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L135: \nL136: Business\nL137:   * cite36†Overview L138:   * cite37†Solutions L139:   * cite38†Resources L140:   * cite39†Plugins L141:   * cite40†Customer Stories L142:   * cite41†Partner Network L143:   * cite11†Contact